// A cyclic `once` THROUGH A FUNCTION CALL, which is the case the lineage
// cons-list exists for — a directly self-referential `once seed = seed` is the
// easy half and a static dependency graph would catch it.
//
// Forcing `seed` calls `read_seed`, which reads `seed` again. The re-entry is
// visible only because the frame the force created is the frame `read_seed`
// received, so the answer is the raw diagnostic on
// stderr and exit 1, with "start" already on stdout.
//
// A `sync.Once`-shaped lowering with no cycle test would DEADLOCK here rather
// than report, which is why rt.OnceCell tests the lineage before taking the
// lock.
import std/io

fn read_seed(): Int {
    seed
}

once seed: Int = read_seed() + 1

fn main() {
    io.print("start")
    io.print("seed ${seed}")
}
